   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend a chapter of the laws of 2020 relating to directing the office\nof fire prevention and control within the division of homeland security and\nemergency services to form a task force and issue a report relating to\nvolunteer firefighter recruitment and retention, as proposed in legislative\nbills numbers S.7589-B and A.9779-A, in relation to the New York state\nvolunteer firefighter recruitment and retention task force and to the\neffectiveness of the provisions thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S864",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S864-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1 & 2, Chap of 2020 (as proposed in S.7589-B & A.9779-A)",
    "lawSection": "Executive Department",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S864\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend a chapter of the laws of 2020 relating to directing the\noffice of fire prevention and control within the division of homeland\nsecurity and emergency services to form a task force and issue a report\nrelating to volunteer firefighter recruitment and retention, as proposed\nin legislative bills numbers S.7589-B and A.9779-A, in relation to the\nNew York state volunteer firefighter recruitment and retention task\nforce and to the effectiveness of the provisions thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2020,\nc.258.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis legislation amends the underlying chapter, which pertains to the\ncreation of a volunteer firefighter recruitment and retention task\nforce, by adding language to:\n\n1) Add an additional nine members appointed by the Governor (in addition\nto the 16 already specified, to make a total of 25);\n\n2) Instead of requiring the task force to report on creating a dedicated\nbureau within the Office of Fire Prevention and Control (OFPC), the task\nforce will study the \"feasibility and necessity\" of creating such\nbureau;\n\n3) Appointments to the task force must be made within 60 days after the\neffective date, rather than 30 under the original legislation; and\n\n4) The act will expire and be deemed repealed on April 1, 2022, instead\nof December 31, 2021.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPursuant to approval memo 12 of 2020, these changes are technical amend-\nments to ensure adequate representation of stakeholders.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter 258 of the Laws of 2020.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as a\nchapter of the laws of 2020 relating to directing the office of fire\nprevention and control within the division of homeland security and\nemergency services to form a task force and issue a report relating to\nvolunteer firefighter recruitment and retention, as proposed in legisla-\ntive bills numbers S.7589-B and A.9779-A, takes effect.\n",
